# Building Figma plugins (figma-plugin-forge)

This repo's `skills/` directory is the canonical knowledge. Each skill has a
`SKILL.md` plus a `references/` folder. When building a Figma plugin, read the
relevant skill instead of guessing.

## Flow (don't skip to code)

1. Brainstorm the idea into a design — `skills/brainstorming/`.
2. Pick the closest pattern — `skills/plugin-architecture/references/patterns/`
   (local-audit, llm-analysis, spec-generation, library-sync).
3. Write a task-by-task plan — `skills/writing-plans/`.
4. Scaffold by copying a verified template verbatim:
   - no backend → `templates/starter-plugin/`
   - with backend → `templates/starter-plugin-backend/`
5. Implement, then verify the Definition of Done.

## Non-negotiable rules

- Runtime split: no `figma.*` in the UI iframe; no DOM / `fetch` / `btoa` in the
  main thread. Network calls happen in the UI.
- Every cross-thread message is a typed, type-guarded plain object — see
  `templates/starter-plugin/src/types/messages.ts` and the bridge helpers.
- `manifest.json`: `documentAccess: "dynamic-page"`; `networkAccess.allowedDomains`
  non-empty and specific (`["none"]` if local-only; never `"*"`).
- Backend code is vendor-agnostic with a local fallback —
  `skills/figma-backend-integration/`.
- API gotchas: `skills/figma-api-patterns/references/common-pitfalls.md`.

## Definition of Done

`npm run lint && npm run typecheck && npm test && npm run build`, then
`node scripts/validate-scaffold.mjs --path <plugin-dir>`.
